1 Open Server Manager on your Windows Server VM and install the Active Directory Certificate Services (AD CS) role, selecting only the Certification Authority role service.
=>Open Server Manager → Manage → Add Roles and Features.
Role-based or feature-based installation → select the local server.
On Server Roles, check Active Directory Certificate Services.
Click Add Features when prompted.
On the Role Services page, select only Certification Authority.
Complete the installation.

2. Configure a new Standalone Root CA using the AD CS Configuration wizard and name the CA after your favorite IPL team (e.g., "GujaratTitansRootCA").
=>Credentials: local Administrator (Standalone) or Enterprise Admin (Enterprise).
Role Services: Certification Authority.
Setup Type:
Standalone CA (if workgroup), or
Enterprise CA (if domain-joined – required for templates).

CA Type: Root CA.
Private Key: Create a new private key.
Cryptography: RSA, SHA256, 2048 or 4096 bits.
CA Name / Common name: e.g. GujaratTitansRootCA (or your preferred IPL team name).
Validity period: 5–15 years (or longer for a root).
Finish the wizard.

3. Create a certificate template for 'App Developers' that allows code signing, then issue a new certificate from your CA using this template and export it as a .cer file.
=>Open certtmpl.msc (Certificate Templates console).
Right-click the built-in Code Signing template → Duplicate Template.
Compatibility: choose a recent Windows Server version.
General tab:
Template display name: App Developers
Template name: AppDevelopers (no spaces).

Request Handling: allow private key to be exported (if needed).
Subject Name: Supply in the request (common for code-signing).
Extensions → Application Policies / Extended Key Usage: ensure Code Signing is present.
Security tab: grant Read + Enroll to the group or users who will request the certificate (e.g., a Developers group).
OK to save the template.
Open certsrv.msc → right-click Certificate Templates → New → Certificate Template to Issue → select App Developers.

4. Simulate a Zomato-style secure API scenario: generate a certificate request (CSR) for an imaginary 'ZomatoAPI' server, submit it to your CA, approve it, and install the issued certificate.
=>certreq -new ZomatoAPI.inf ZomatoAPI.req
  Submit the request to the CA:
Via Web Enrollment (http://<CA>/certsrv) if installed, or
certreq -submit -config "CAServer\GujaratTitansRootCA" ZomatoAPI.req ZomatoAPI.cer
Or copy the .req file to the CA and use the CA console.

On the CA (certsrv.msc):
Go to Pending Requests.
Right-click the request → Issue.

Retrieve and install the issued certificate:
Export from Issued Certificates (or use certreq -retrieve).
On the ZomatoAPI server, import the certificate (with private key if it was generated there) into the Local Computer → Personal store and bind it to the HTTPS site / API endpoint.

5. Use ChatGPT or Copilot to generate a PowerShell script that lists all issued certificates from your CA, then run the script and take a screenshot of the output.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Ask the AI tool for a script using Get-CertificationAuthority and Get-IssuedRequest cmdlets.</em>4
=># Install once (requires internet or offline copy)
Install-Module -Name PSPKI -Scope CurrentUser -Force

Import-Module PSPKI

# List all issued certificates
Get-CertificationAuthority | Get-IssuedRequest |
    Select-Object RequestID, RequesterName, CommonName, CertificateTemplate,
                  SerialNumber, NotBefore, NotAfter, CertificateEffectiveDate |
    Format-Table -AutoSize
